L1: Bioavailability of vitamin C from kiwifruit in non-smoking males:
L2: determination of ‘healthy’ and ‘optimal’ intakes
L3: Journal of Nutritional Science (2012)
L4: Anitra C. Carr, Juliet M. Pullar, Stephanie Moran, Margreet C. M. Vissers
L5: Centre for Free Radical Research, Department of
L6: Pathology ,  University of Otago ,
L7:  Christchurch, PO Box 4345, Christchurch ,  New
L8: Zealand
L9: Vitamin C is an essential nutrient in humans and must be obtained through the diet. The
L10: aim of this study was to determine vitamin C uptake in healthy volunteers after consuming
L11: kiwifruit ( Actinidia chinensis  var.  Hort. 16A ), and to
L12: determine the amount of fruit required to raise plasma vitamin C to ‘healthy’ (i.e.
L13: >50 µmol/l) and ‘optimal’ or saturating levels (i.e. >70 µmol/l). Leucocyte
L14: and urinary vitamin C levels were also determined. A total of fifteen male university
L15: students with below average levels of plasma vitamin C were selected for the study. Weekly
L16: fasting blood samples were obtained for a 4-week lead-in period and following
L17: supplementation with, sequentially, half, one, two and three Gold kiwifruit per d for 4–6
L18: weeks each, followed by a final 4-week washout period. The results showed that addition of
L19: as little as half a kiwifruit per d resulted in a significant increase in plasma vitamin
L20: C. However, one kiwifruit per d was required to reach what is considered healthy levels.
L21: Increasing the dose of kiwifruit to two per d resulted in further increases in plasma
L22: vitamin C levels as well as increased urinary output of the vitamin, indicating that
L23: plasma levels were saturating at this dosage. Dividing the participants into high and low
L24: vitamin C groups based on their baseline plasma and leucocyte vitamin C levels
L25: demonstrated that it is critical to obtain a study population with low initial levels of
L26: the vitamin in order to ascertain a consistent effect of supplementation.
L27: Human and Clinical Nutrition
L28: Plasma vitamin C; Human saturation levels; Kiwifruit supplementation; Leucocytes; DTPA, diethylene triamine pentaacetic acid; RDI, recommended dietary intake
L29: Introduction
L30: Vitamin C (ascorbate) is an essential nutrient in humans ( 
L31:  1 
L32:  ) , and insufficient dietary intake leads to the potentially fatal
L33: deficiency disease, scurvy ( 
L34:  2 
L35:  ) . Vitamin C has a number of important functions  in vivo .
L36: It is an essential cofactor for a variety of dioxygenase enzymes that hydroxylate amino
L37: acids in the synthesis of pro-collagen, carnitine, hormones and
L38: neurotransmitters ( 
L39:  3 
L40:  )  and is also a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant, scavenging
L41: both one-electron and two-electron oxidants ( 
L42:  4 
L43:  ) . However, the significance of the antioxidant activity  in
L44: vivo  remains to be determined. Recent research has uncovered vital novel functions
L45: for members of the dioxygenase family, including roles in gene regulation and signalling
L46: pathways ( 
L47:  5 
L48:  , 
L49:  6 
L50:  ) . Vitamin C is now known to be a cofactor for the hydroxylases
L51: responsible for the regulation of the transcription factor hypoxia-inducible factor
L52: 1 ( 
L53:  7 
L54:  – 
L55:  10 
L56:  ) , a metabolic sensor that has been implicated in a number of conditions
L57: such as cancer, ischaemic cardiovascular disorders and inflammation ( 
L58:  9 
L59:  , 
L60:  11 
L61:  ) . As hypoxia-inducible factor 1 is ubiquitously expressed throughout the
L62: body, there is a requirement for adequate levels of vitamin C in all tissues.
L63: Numerous epidemiological studies have shown that high dietary intakes and/or high plasma
L64: levels of vitamin C are associated with a decreased risk of CVD and cerebrovascular disease
L65: and cancer (reviewed in Carr & Frei ( 
L66:  12 
L67:  ) ). It is possible that vitamin C may simply be a marker for high fruit
L68: and vegetable intake ( 
L69:  13 
L70:  )  and other plant-derived components may be responsible for the observed
L71: health effects. However, based on vitamin C's newly discovered involvement in gene
L72: regulation and signalling, it is likely that the vitamin has an essential role in
L73: maintaining human health and preventing disease.
L74: The current Australasian recommended dietary intake (RDI) of vitamin C is 45 mg/d for
L75: non-smoking men and women ( 
L76:  14 
L77:  ) . Pharmacokinetic studies carried out by Levine  et
L78: al. ( 
L79:  15 
L80:  , 
L81:  16 
L82:  )  indicate that this intake of vitamin C provides a plasma level which is
L83: considered ‘marginal’ or ‘inadequate’, i.e. <23 µmol/l ( 
L84:  17 
L85:  – 
L86:  19 
L87:  ) . Thus, the optimal intake of vitamin C required to maintain general
L88: health and wellbeing may be higher than the current RDI and is probably closer to a dose at
L89: which plasma reaches saturation, i.e. about 70 µmol/l. Along these lines, we and
L90: others ( 
L91:  12 
L92:  , 
L93:  15 
L94:  , 
L95:  16 
L96:  )  have recommended a vitamin C intake of 120–200 mg/d, which can be
L97: obtained from a diet containing the recommended five plus daily servings of fruit and
L98: vegetables. It is known that there is a significant proportion of the population that does
L99: not achieve these intakes and whose plasma vitamin C levels are well below the recommended
L100: optimum ( 
L101:  20 
L102:  , 
L103:  21 
L104:  ) .
L105: Although vitamin C is one of the most frequently used dietary supplements ( 
L106:  22 
L107:  ) , a food source of the vitamin may be preferred due to the presence of
L108: other potentially beneficial or synergistic constituents. Zespri ®  Gold kiwifruit
L109: ( Actinidia chinensis  var.  Hort 16A ) are an outstanding
L110: commonly available source of vitamin C, one serving providing twice the current Australasian
L111: RDI of the vitamin ( 
L112:  23 
L113:  ) . We recently completed an animal study, using a genetically vitamin
L114: C-deficient mouse model (the Gulo mouse), investigating the uptake of vitamin C from
L115: kiwifruit gel compared with vitamin C-supplemented water ( 
L116:  24 
L117:  ) . Interestingly, we found that kiwifruit provided significantly higher
L118: serum and tissue levels of vitamin C than did supplemented water. This suggests some type of
L119: synergistic activity of the whole fruit in this mouse model.
L120: The primary aim of this study was to determine the uptake of vitamin C from kiwifruit in
L121: young men with inadequate vitamin C intake, in order to determine the amount of fruit
L122: required to raise plasma vitamin C to ‘healthy’ (i.e. >50 µmol/l) and ‘optimal’ or
L123: saturating levels (i.e. >70 µmol/l) ( 
L124:  17 
L125:  ) . Several studies have measured plasma uptake of vitamin C from kiwifruit
L126: in healthy volunteers ( 
L127:  25 
L128:  – 
L129:  27 
L130:  ) , in women with low Fe stores ( 
L131:  28 
L132:  ) , in hyperlipidaemic individuals ( 
L133:  29 
L134:  )  and in smokers ( 
L135:  30 
L136:  ) . However, in these studies the participants already had high or
L137: saturating levels of plasma vitamin C at baseline (i.e. about 50–70 µmol/l), thus abrogating
L138: an obvious or consistent effect of supplementation. As such, a major objective of this study
L139: was to select individuals with low or below average plasma vitamin C in order to be able to
L140: observe a clear effect of supplementation. We monitored the dietary intake, plasma uptake
L141: and urinary excretion of the vitamin following supplementation with, sequentially, half,
L142: one, two and three Gold kiwifruit per d for 4–6 weeks each, and also measured leucocyte
L143: vitamin C levels as an indicator of tissue levels.
L144: Experimental methods
L145: Participants
L146: This study was conducted according to the guidelines laid down in the Declaration of
L147: Helsinki and all procedures involving human subjects were approved by the Upper South
L148: Regional Ethics Committee (consent no. URB/10/06/020). Written informed consent was
L149: obtained from all participants. Non-smoking males aged 18–30 years were recruited from
L150: local universities for the screening phase of the study.
L151: A total of sixty students underwent a screening interview to ascertain their eligibility
L152: for the study. Anthropometric measurements were carried out to determine their BMI and a
L153: fasting venous blood sample was drawn to determine their plasma vitamin C levels.
L154: Exclusion criteria included: recent smoker (within the previous year), allergy/intolerance
L155: to kiwifruit, taking vitamin C-containing supplements (within the past 3 months), taking
L156: prescription medication (within the past 3 months), excessive alcohol consumption (more
L157: than twenty-one standard drinks per week), high fruit and vegetable consumption (more than
L158: five servings per d), current vegetarian or vegan, diabetes mellitus, bleeding disorders,
L159: obese (BMI > 35 kg/m 2 ) and fainting due to fear of needles.
L160: Power calculations indicated that at 95 % power with a 5 % significance level, a sample
L161: size of nine was adequate for detecting a minimum difference of 30 ( sd  25) µmol/l
L162: as determined from the data presented in Levine  et al. ( 
L163:  15 
L164:  ) . However, to allow for potential withdrawal due to the length of the
L165: study, fifteen non-smoking participants with less than average baseline plasma vitamin C
L166: levels were enrolled. One participant withdrew after completion of the lead-in phase and
L167: was not included in subsequent baseline calculations.
L168: Study design
L169: Fig. 1. Study design which consisted of a lead-in phase of 4 weeks, an intervention phase
L170: of 20 weeks and a washout phase of 4 weeks. * Fasting blood samples taken. † 24 h
L171: urine collection and total leucocyte preparations carried out and when food and
L172: beverage diaries completed.
L173: The study comprised a 4-week lead-in phase, an intervention phase of 20 weeks and a
L174: 4-week washout phase ( Fig. 1 ). During the lead-in
L175: phase, weekly fasting blood samples were drawn to monitor variability in plasma vitamin C
L176: levels. At week 4 of the lead-in phase, baseline levels were measured in plasma and total
L177: leucocytes as described later and a 24 h urine collection was carried out to determine
L178: urinary vitamin C excretion. Participants also completed daily food and drink diaries to
L179: monitor their dietary vitamin C intake as indicated in  Fig. 1 .  Fig. 1. Study design which consisted of a lead-in phase of 4 weeks, an intervention phase
L180: of 20 weeks and a washout phase of 4 weeks. * Fasting blood samples taken. † 24 h
L181: urine collection and total leucocyte preparations carried out and when food and
L182: beverage diaries completed.
L183: At the beginning of the intervention phase, participants were asked to consume half a
L184: kiwifruit per d for 4 weeks, followed by one kiwifruit per d for 6 weeks, two kiwifruit
L185: per d for 6 weeks and finally three kiwifruit per d for 4 weeks ( Fig. 1 ). A longer time period was chosen for the one and two
L186: kiwifruit per d doses to allow the two kiwifruit per d dose to span the 2-week Christmas
L187: and New Year break, i.e. participants did not come into the clinic for blood tests during
L188: this time, but were provided with additional kiwifruit to allow them to remain on the
L189: appropriate dose for the duration of this holiday period. Fasting blood samples were drawn
L190: weekly for vitamin C analysis and additional analyses were carried out on 24 h urine
L191: samples and total leucocyte preparations at the end of each dose of kiwifruit. For each
L192: dose of kiwifruit, participants completed a daily food diary for 1 week.
L193: During the final washout phase of the study the participants returned to their normal
L194: diet for 4 weeks ( Fig. 1 ). Blood samples were
L195: drawn weekly and a 24 h urine collection and a total leucocyte preparation were carried
L196: out at the end of the washout. A 1-week daily food and drink record was completed to
L197: monitor dietary vitamin C intake at the end of the washout phase.
L198: Intervention
L199: Zespri ®  Gold kiwifruit ( A. chinensis  var.  Hort.
L200: 16A ) were stored at ≤4°C. The vitamin C content was monitored by extracting a
L201: sample of kiwifruit in 0·54  m -perchloric acid containing the metal chelator
L202: diethylene triamine pentaacetic acid (DTPA) (100 µmol/l) followed by
L203: centrifugation ( 
L204:  24 
L205:  ) . The supernatant was analysed by HPLC with electrochemical detection
L206: as described later. There was 89 ( sd  8) mg vitamin C/100 g fruit
L207: ( n  4), which is equivalent to about 80 mg vitamin C per kiwifruit.
L208: Sample collection and processing
L209: Plasma
L210: Peripheral blood was collected into 5 ml K 3 -EDTA vacutainer tubes to
L211: stabilise the vitamin C ( 
L212:  31 
L213:  )  and immediately placed on melting ice. Samples were centrifuged at
L214: 4°C, plasma removed and added to an equal volume of ice-cold 0·54  m -HPLC-grade
L215: perchloric acid/DTPA solution to precipitate the protein and stabilise the vitamin C.
L216: The perchloric acid/DTPA extracts were centrifuged and the deproteinated supernatants
L217: were stored at −80°C until HPLC analysis.
L218: Urine
L219: Urine was collected over 24 h into bottles containing K 2 -EDTA (final
L220: concentration 100 µmol/l) to stabilise the vitamin C ( 
L221:  32 
L222:  ) . Samples were diluted twofold with an ice-cold
L223: 0·54  m -HPLC-grade perchloric acid/DTPA solution and centrifuged before storage
L224: of the supernatants as described earlier.
L225: Leucocytes
L226: Total leucocytes were purified from 20 ml whole blood by dextran sedimentation and
L227: hypotonic lysis to remove erythrocytes as described previously ( 
L228:  33 
L229:  ) . The isolated cells were counted using a haemocytometer and vitamin
L230: C extracts of pelleted cells were prepared with an ice-cold 0·54  m -HPLC-grade
L231: perchloric acid/DTPA solution. The protein content was removed by centrifugation and the
L232: supernatants were stored as described earlier.
L233: Analysis of vitamin C by HPLC
L234: The vitamin C content of the kiwifruit, plasma, urine and leucocytes was analysed using
L235: reverse-phase HPLC with electrochemical detection using a modified method of Lee
L236:  et al. ( 
L237:  34 
L238:  ) . Samples were analysed weekly and were separated on a Synergi 4 µ
L239: Hydro-RP 80A column 150 × 4·6 mm (Phenomenex NZ Ltd) using a Waters 600 solvent delivery
L240: system with a Hitachi L-2200 refrigerated autosampler and an ESA Coulochem II detector
L241: (+200 mV electrode potential and 20 µA sensitivity). The mobile phase comprised
L242: 80 m m -sodium acetate buffer, pH 4·8, containing DTPA (0·54 mmol/l) and a freshly
L243: added paired-ion reagent  n -octylamine (1 µmol/l), delivered at a flow
L244: rate of 1·2 ml/min. Freshly prepared milli-q water was used for the preparation of all
L245: reagents and the mobile phase was sparged with helium. Each sample was analysed in
L246: duplicate or triplicate and the vitamin C content was determined to be stable for the
L247: duration of the analyses. The stability of the samples was determined under all storage
L248: conditions and no loss of ascorbate was observed. A standard curve of
L249: sodium- l -ascorbate, standardised spectrophotometrically at 245 nm (ɛ = 9860), was
L250: freshly prepared for each HPLC run in 77 mmol/l HPLC-grade perchloric acid containing DTPA
L251: (100 µmol/l). Plasma vitamin C content is expressed as μmol/l, urinary vitamin C content
L252: is expressed as mg/24 h and total leucocyte vitamin C content is expressed as
L253: nmol/10 8  cells.
L254: Analysis of food and beverage records
L255: The participants recorded their daily food and beverage intake for 1 week for each phase
L256: of the study (i.e. at baseline, half a kiwifruit per d, one kiwifruit per d, two kiwifruit
L257: per d, three kiwifruit per d and washout). They provided a detailed description of each
L258: item, including components of mixed dishes, brand of item, preparation or cooking process,
L259: and quantified the items using standard household measures, metric weights or volumes,
L260: size of food item or standard serves ( 
L261:  35 
L262:  ) . Food diary entries were followed up with the participants to confirm
L263: the accuracy of reporting. The number of servings of fruit and vegetables consumed by each
L264: participant was estimated from their food and beverage records using New Zealand Ministry
L265: of Health guidelines ( 
L266:  36 
L267:  ) . The vitamin C content of the fruit- and vegetable-containing foods
L268: and beverages was estimated using Diet Cruncher software (version 1.6, Way Down South
L269: Software) and the New Zealand FOODfiles Food Composition Database (2006).
L270: Statistical analysis
L271: Data are represented as either mean ( sd ) or mean ( sem ), as indicated in
L272: the text, and  P  ≤ 0·05 was considered significant. One-way
L273: repeated-measures ANOVA with the Fisher least significant deviation pairwise multiple
L274: comparison procedure was carried out using Sigma Stat software (version 11, Systat
L275: Software Inc.). The differences between paired data were determined by the two-tailed
L276: paired  t  test. The numbers of participants at each data point are
L277: indicated in the figures and tables.
L278: Results
L279: Screening
L280: A total of sixty non-smoking male university students were screened for this study ( Table 1 ). Their mean fasting plasma vitamin C level
L281: was 49 ( sd  16) µmol/l, with a range of 6·8–77 µmol/l. Of those screened, fifteen
L282: young men with less than average plasma vitamin C levels, who also satisfied the other
L283: inclusion/exclusion criteria, were enrolled in the study. The mean fasting plasma vitamin
L284: C level of the enrolled group was 31 ( sd  11) µmol/l, with a range of
L285: 8·8–44 µmol/l. Other than plasma vitamin C, there were no differences in the
L286: characteristics between the screened and the enrolled individuals ( Table 1 ). During the course of the study, four participants withdrew
L287: before the end of the intervention phase and three did not complete the washout phase.
L288:  Table 1. Characteristics of individuals screened and enrolled in the study (Mean values, standard deviations and ranges) Screened ( n  60) Enrolled ( n  15) Mean 
