L302: Fresh fruit and vegetables are the major dietary source of vitamin C. The number of fruit
L303: and vegetable servings consumed per d by the study participants, as determined from their
L304: food and beverage records, indicated a mean consumption of 3·9 ( sem  0·5) servings
L305: of fruit and vegetables per d by the end of the lead-in period. Addition of kiwifruit to
L306: the diet resulted in an increase in fruit and vegetable consumption during the two and
L307: three kiwifruit per d supplementation phases of the study, with the average intake
L308: approaching six serves per d at the three kiwifruit dosage ( Fig. 2(a) ). The non-kiwifruit component of the diet was about three
L309: serves for the duration of the supplementation period. Consumption of fruit and vegetables
L310: returned to baseline levels during the washout period.  Fig. 2. (a) Daily fruit and vegetable consumption and (b) vitamin C intake by the study
L311: participants. (■), Total fruit and vegetable intake or vitamin C intake;
L312: ( ), total minus kiwifruit intervention.
L313: Data are means, with standard errors represented by vertical bars. The numbers of
L314: participants are indicated in parentheses. * Mean value was significantly different
L315: from that at baseline ( P  < 0·05; one-way repeated-measures
L316: ANOVA with the Fisher least significant deviation pairwise multiple comparison
L317: procedure). WO, washout.
L318: Analysis of the participants' daily vitamin C intake suggested a mean baseline intake of
L319: 44 ( sem  5) mg/d of vitamin C. Consuming kiwifruit had a dose-dependent effect on
L320: daily vitamin C intake, with a significant increase from a dosage of half a kiwifruit per
L321: d onwards ( Fig. 2 (b)). When participants were
L322: given three kiwifruit per d, the average daily intake of vitamin C reached about 300 mg/d.
L323: During the 4-week washout period the participants' daily vitamin C intake returned to
L324: baseline levels.
L325: Plasma vitamin C
L326: Fig. 3. Weekly plasma vitamin C levels of the combined group (▼;  n  14),
L327: low vitamin C participants (•;  n  7) and high vitamin C participants
L328: (■;  n  7). Data are means, with standard errors represented by
L329: vertical bars. * Mean value was significantly different from that at baseline
L330: ( P  < 0·05; one-way repeated-measures ANOVA with the Fisher
L331: least significant deviation pairwise multiple comparison procedure).
L332: Fig. 4. (a) Plasma vitamin C levels of study participants and (b) urinary excretion of
L333: vitamin C by the study participants as a function of daily kiwifruit (KF) intake.
L334: Data are means, with standard errors represented by vertical bars. The numbers of
L335: participants are indicated in parentheses. * Mean value was significantly different
L336: from that at baseline ( P  < 0·05; one-way repeated-measures
L337: ANOVA with the Fisher least significant deviation pairwise multiple comparison
L338: procedure). WO, washout. (c) Correlation of plasma vitamin C with urinary excretion
L339: of vitamin C. Data points ( n  62) were obtained from participants at
L340: each stage of the study (baseline, 0·5 KF/d, 1 KF/d, 2 KF/d, 3 KF/d, WO).
L341: The participants' mean baseline fasting plasma vitamin C concentration was 38
L342: ( sem  4) µmol/l ( Fig. 3 ). After 3 weeks of
L343: supplementation with half a kiwifruit per d there was a significant increase in plasma
L344: vitamin C level to 49 µmol/l ( P  = 0·010), with a maximum level of
L345: 50 µmol/l observed by the fourth week ( Figs. 3  and
L346:  4 (a)). Increasing the dose to one kiwifruit per
L347: d increased the average plasma vitamin C to 53 µmol/l after 6 weeks, and two kiwifruit per
L348: d for the next 6 weeks resulted in a further increase to 62 µmol/l ( Fig. 4 (a)). There was a significant difference between the one and
L349: two kiwifruit per d doses ( P  = 0·030). The final dose of three kiwifruit
L350: per d initially resulted in a maximal average plasma vitamin C of 68 µmol/l, an increase
L351: of 32 µmol/l above baseline, although after 4 weeks the average vitamin C level had
L352: dropped back to 58 µmol/l ( Fig. 4(a) ), with a high
L353: degree of variability in plasma vitamin C levels at this dosage. Following a 4-week
L354: washout the participants' plasma vitamin C levels had dropped back to 42 µmol/l, close to
L355: their starting level of 38 µmol/l.  Fig. 3. Weekly plasma vitamin C levels of the combined group (▼;  n  14),
L356: low vitamin C participants (•;  n  7) and high vitamin C participants
L357: (■;  n  7). Data are means, with standard errors represented by
L358: vertical bars. * Mean value was significantly different from that at baseline
L359: ( P  < 0·05; one-way repeated-measures ANOVA with the Fisher
L360: least significant deviation pairwise multiple comparison procedure). 
L361:  Fig. 4. (a) Plasma vitamin C levels of study participants and (b) urinary excretion of
L362: vitamin C by the study participants as a function of daily kiwifruit (KF) intake.
L363: Data are means, with standard errors represented by vertical bars. The numbers of
L364: participants are indicated in parentheses. * Mean value was significantly different
L365: from that at baseline ( P  < 0·05; one-way repeated-measures
L366: ANOVA with the Fisher least significant deviation pairwise multiple comparison
L367: procedure). WO, washout. (c) Correlation of plasma vitamin C with urinary excretion
L368: of vitamin C. Data points ( n  62) were obtained from participants at
L369: each stage of the study (baseline, 0·5 KF/d, 1 KF/d, 2 KF/d, 3 KF/d, WO).
L370: Preliminary statistical analysis indicated that the plasma vitamin C data were bimodal.
L371: Therefore, the participants were divided into two groups around their baseline mean at
L372: week 4, i.e. 37 µmol/l ( Fig. 3 ). No significant
L373: increase in plasma vitamin C was observed for the higher group (with a baseline mean of
L374: 50 µmol/l vitamin C). In contrast, the lower group (with a baseline mean of 26 µmol/l
L375: vitamin C) showed an earlier response to supplementation as compared with the combined
L376: group, with a significant increase observed following the first week on half a kiwifruit
L377: per d ( Fig. 3 ). A maximum average plasma vitamin C
L378: concentration of 67 µmol/l was reached in the lower group after 6 weeks of supplementation
L379: with two kiwifruit per d, an increase of 43 µmol/l above baseline.
L380: Urinary vitamin C
L381: Urinary vitamin C levels were measured in order to monitor excretion of the vitamin. The
L382: mean baseline vitamin C level was 29 ( sem  10) mg/24 h ( Fig. 4(b) ) and these levels remained unchanged until the two
L383: kiwifruit dosage, when a significant increase in urinary vitamin C level to 109
L384: ( sem  30) mg/24 h was observed ( Fig.
L385: 4(b) ). This suggests that plasma saturation is not occurring until this dosage.
L386: Urinary vitamin C levels at the three kiwifruit dosage were highly variable. Following the
L387: 4-week washout period the urinary vitamin C values dropped back to baseline. Correlation
L388: of plasma vitamin C with urinary vitamin C indicated a marked increase in urinary output
L389: of the vitamin only at plasma concentrations of >60 µmol/l vitamin C ( Fig. 4(c) ). This was generally achieved by our
L390: participants when they consumed two or three kiwifruit per d and indicates that plasma
L391: levels are a more accurate measure of saturation than the amount of fruit consumed.
L392: Leucocyte vitamin C
L393: Fig. 5. (a) Leucocyte vitamin C levels of the low vitamin C group as a function of daily
L394: kiwifruit (KF) intake. Data are means, with standard errors represented by vertical
L395: bars. The numbers of participants are indicated in parentheses. * Mean value was
L396: significantly different from that at baseline ( P  < 0·05;
L397: two-tailed paired  t  test). WO, washout. (b) Correlation of plasma
L398: vitamin C with leucocyte vitamin C. Data points ( n  58) were
L399: obtained from participants at each stage of the study (baseline, 0·5 KF/d, 1 KF/d, 2
L400: KF/d, 3 KF/d, WO). Linear regression analysis provided an  R  value
L401: of 0·374 and a  P  value of 0·004.
L402: The baseline vitamin C level of the participants' leucocytes was 49 ( sem 
L403: 3) nmol/10 8  cells. There was a small increase in leucocyte vitamin C levels
L404: with intervention, with the two kiwifruit per d dosage reaching 53 ( sem 
L405: 5) nmol/10 8  cells ( P  = 0·057). However, when the
L406: participants were divided into two groups around their baseline mean at week 4, the low
L407: vitamin C group (39 ( sem  3) nmol/10 8  cells) showed a significant
L408: increase in vitamin C at both the one and two kiwifruit per d dosages ( Fig. 5(a) ). Interestingly, there was a significant
L409: drop in leucocyte vitamin C at the three kiwifruit per d dosage, which may reflect the
L410: drop in plasma levels in this subgroup. A significant correlation of plasma vitamin C with
L411: leucocyte vitamin C was observed ( R  0·374,  P  = 0·004)
L412: ( Fig. 5(b) ).  Fig. 5. (a) Leucocyte vitamin C levels of the low vitamin C group as a function of daily
L413: kiwifruit (KF) intake. Data are means, with standard errors represented by vertical
L414: bars. The numbers of participants are indicated in parentheses. * Mean value was
L415: significantly different from that at baseline ( P  < 0·05;
L416: two-tailed paired  t  test). WO, washout. (b) Correlation of plasma
L417: vitamin C with leucocyte vitamin C. Data points ( n  58) were
L418: obtained from participants at each stage of the study (baseline, 0·5 KF/d, 1 KF/d, 2
L419: KF/d, 3 KF/d, WO). Linear regression analysis provided an  R  value
L420: of 0·374 and a  P  value of 0·004.
L421: Discussion
L422: A significant downfall of many supplementation studies is the high initial vitamin C status
L423: of the participants, which can abrogate a clear effect of supplementation on the plasma
L424: vitamin levels ( 
L425:  25 
L426:  – 
L427:  30 
L428:  ) . Therefore, a major objective of this study was to recruit individuals
L429: with low initial vitamin C status. We screened sixty male university students to determine
L430: their vitamin C status and enrolled fifteen of the lowest for the study. Despite thirteen of
L431: these fifteen individuals already having what is considered by some to be ‘adequate’ plasma
L432: levels of the vitamin (i.e. >23 µmol/l) ( 
L433:  17 
L434:  – 
L435:  19 
L436:  ) , supplementation of the participants with as little as half a kiwifruit
L437: per d resulted in a significant increase in their plasma vitamin C levels by the third week.
L438: Supplementation with one, two and three kiwifruit per d resulted in further increases in
L439: plasma vitamin C levels.
L440: The primary aim of this study was to investigate the effect of a high vitamin C-containing
L441: fruit such as kiwifruit on plasma vitamin C levels and specifically to determine the dosage
L442: required to reach ‘healthy’ and ‘optimal’ levels. Considerable debate exists as to what
L443: constitutes ‘healthy’ or ‘optimal’ intake, as evidenced by the differing RDI for the
L444: vitamin; in Australasia 45 mg/d is considered adequate for both men and women whereas the
L445: USA and Canada recommend 90 mg/d for men and 75 mg/d for women. The current understanding is
L446: that a dose which gives a saturated plasma level (about 70 µmol/l) is ‘optimal’, as this is
L447: likely to provide additional health benefits of vitamin C beyond preventing
L448: scurvy ( 
L449:  17 
L450:  , 
L451:  37 
L452:  ) . In our study, enhanced urinary output of vitamin C was not observed
L453: until plasma levels were >60 µmol/l, suggesting that plasma saturation occurs at this
L454: level. We found that this required the addition of two kiwifruit per d to the diet, with an
L455: overall daily intake of around 220 mg vitamin C. Levine  et al.  also
L456: reported plasma saturation at a vitamin C intake of about 200 mg/d ( 
L457:  15 
L458:  , 
L459:  16 
L460:  ) .
L461: It has been difficult to define what constitutes a ‘healthy’ plasma level of vitamin C.
L462: Clinical guidelines of severe vitamin C deficiency (plasma <11 µmol/l) and marginal
L463: vitamin C deficiency (plasma <23 µmol/l) have long been established and relate to the
L464: risk of developing scurvy ( 
L465:  17 
L466:  , 
L467:  19 
L468:  ) . Lykkesfeldt & Poulsen ( 
L469:  17 
L470:  )  recently proposed a new category of suboptimal vitamin C status which is
L471: based on the supposition that if a plasma concentration of 70 µmol/l and above is ‘optimal,’
L472: then those individuals with plasma concentrations between 23 and 50 µmol/l have plasma
L473: concentrations which are suboptimal. The clinical significance of the suboptimal category
L474: has not yet been clarified; however, we and others ( 
L475:  38 
L476:  )  have measured a significant correlation between leucocyte and plasma
L477: vitamin C levels, suggesting that tissue levels respond to increased intake and optimal
L478: plasma concentrations. In our study, plasma concentrations of 50 µmol/l and above were
L479: obtained by supplementation of individuals with a minimum of one kiwifruit per d. This is
L480: readily achievable as part of a balanced diet.
L481: When the study participants were divided into two groups around their mean plasma vitamin C
L482: level, those participants with the lowest level at baseline (mean of 26 µmol/l vitamin C)
L483: showed an earlier and greater response to supplementation, with half to three kiwifruit per
L484: d giving increases in plasma vitamin C levels ranging from 22 to 43 µmol/l. This compares
L485: with the increases observed for the combined data of 14–32 µmol/l. Collins  et
L486: al. ( 
L487:  25 
L488:  )  showed a more modest dose-dependent increase in plasma vitamin C, with
L489: increases from 7 to 16 µmol/l, following supplementation with one, two and three kiwifruit
L490: per d. However, in that study, participants were not selected on the basis of low vitamin C
L491: and had reasonably high levels prior to supplementation. In comparison with the low group,
L492: the high baseline vitamin C group (mean plasma levels of 50 µmol/l) showed little effect of
L493: supplementation. This lack of a dose-dependent effect with the high group is consistent with
L494: the observations of two previous kiwifruit supplementation studies ( 
L495:  26 
L496:  , 
L497:  27 
L498:  ) , and probably reflects the high initial vitamin C status of the
L499: participants. If plasma levels are approaching saturation it will be difficult to
L500: demonstrate an effect, as any excess will be excreted ( 
L501:  15 
L502:  , 
L503:  17 
L504:  ) . Our study therefore provides a clear demonstration of the importance of
L505: recruiting a suitable population for supplementation studies.
L506: Leucocytes readily accumulate vitamin C and our results support the earlier findings of
L507: Levine  et al. ( 
L508:  15 
L509:  , 
L510:  16 
L511:  ) , who reported saturation of leucocytes at an intake of 100 mg/d. We
L512: observed an increase in total blood leucocyte vitamin C levels in the low vitamin C
L513: participants with the consumption of one and two kiwifruit per d and an increase in the
L514: combined group at a dosage of two kiwifruit per d. Previous studies in which leucocytes were
L515: separated into mononuclear cells (monocytes and lymphocytes) and granulocytes (neutrophils)
L516: have indicated a more variable dose-dependent effect with mononuclear cell vitamin C levels
L517: than with granulocytes ( 
L518:  15 
L519:  , 
L520:  16 
L521:  , 
L522:  38 
L523:  ) . Since mononuclear cells comprise about 40 % of leucocyte samples and
L524: contain two to three times the amount of vitamin C as granulocytes, using a total blood
L525: leucocyte preparation may abrogate an obvious dose-dependent effect of supplementation.
L526: The participants' baseline daily vitamin C intake was 44 mg/d, which is close to the
L527: Australasian RDI of 45 mg/d, and indicates a vitamin C intake of about 11 mg/serving of
L528: fruit and vegetables. Despite the lack of contribution of half a kiwifruit per d to the
L529: participants' average number of fruit and vegetable servings, this dose did nevertheless
L530: result in a significant increase in their average daily vitamin C intake (to about 80 mg/d)
L531: and did significantly increase plasma levels. The one kiwifruit per d dose resulted in an
L532: average vitamin C intake of 130 mg/d, which is close to the intake recommended to prevent
L533: chronic disease, i.e. a ‘healthy’ intake ( 
L534:  12 
L535:  ) . The dosage of two kiwifruit per d resulted in an average vitamin C
L536: intake of 220 mg/d, which is comparable with the dose recommended to reach plasma
L537: saturation, i.e. an ‘optimal’ intake ( 
L538:  37 
L539:  ) . We observed that some of the participants incorporated the kiwifruit
L540: intervention into their daily diet by reducing their intake of other fruits and vegetables,
L541: while other participants consumed the kiwifruit in addition to their normal daily diet.
L542: Interestingly, the participants who incorporated the kiwifruit intervention into their
L543: baseline diet still exhibited a significant increase in their daily vitamin C intake with
L544: intervention. This reflects the significantly higher amount of vitamin C in kiwifruit
L545: compared with many other fruits and vegetables and emphasises the effectiveness of consuming
L546: a food with high vitamin C content.
L547: Somewhat surprisingly, there was a drop in plasma and urinary vitamin C levels after 4
L548: weeks on three kiwifruit per d, although these levels did not differ significantly from two
L549: kiwifruit per d. The variability in plasma and urinary vitamin C levels at this higher
L550: dosage probably reflects a degree of non-compliance by some of the participants. This is
L551: likely to be the case, as meta-analysis has indicated that plasma vitamin C reflects dietary
L552: intake ( 
L553:  39 
L554:  ) . Another possible reason for the observed decrease is the inhibition of
L555: intestinal uptake of vitamin C by kiwifruit-derived flavonoids ( 
L556:  40 
L557:  ) . Similarly, the significant drop in leucocyte vitamin C levels at the
L558: three kiwifruit per d dosage probably reflects participant non-compliance at this dosage or
L559: possible inhibition of cellular vitamin C uptake by kiwifruit-derived
L560: flavonoids ( 
L561:  41 
L562:  ) .
L563: Several studies have suggested that kiwifruit consumption has significant biological
L564: effects. In addition to containing high concentrations of vitamin C, kiwifruit are also a
L565: good source of vitamins E and K, folate, K, fibre, carotenoids and polyphenols, and these
L566: compounds may also confer health benefits. Kiwifruit have been shown to improve digestive
L567: health ( 
L568:  42 
L569:  – 
L570:  44 
L571:  ) , modulate lipid profiles ( 
L572:  26 
L573:  , 
L574:  29 
L575:  )  and reduce platelet aggregation ( 
L576:  26 
L577:  , 
L578:  27 
L579:  , 
L580:  45 
L581:  ) . There is some evidence that consumption of one to three kiwifruit per d
L582: reduces endogenous levels of oxidised pyrimidines and purines in the DNA of healthy
L583: individuals ( 
L584:  25 
L585:  , 
L586:  27 
L587:  )  and affects DNA repair activity ( 
L588:  25 
L589:  , 
L590:  27 
L591:  , 
L592:  46 
L593:  ) . Bohn  et al . demonstrated up-regulation of a number of
L594: DNA repair genes in blood cells from male smokers supplemented with three kiwifruit per d
L595: for 8 weeks ( 
L596:  47 
L597:  ) . Consumption of Gold kiwifruit has also been shown to improve Fe
L598: absorption in women with low-Fe status ( 
L599:  28 
L600:  ) , probably due to the ability of vitamin C to enhance the absorption of
L601: non-haem Fe.
L602: Overall, the results of our human study show kiwifruit to be an excellent source of vitamin
L603: C in humans. Addition to the daily diet of as little as half a kiwifruit resulted in a
L604: significant increase in plasma vitamin C in men who, despite consuming up to four serves of
L605: fruit and vegetables per d, still had below average vitamin C status. However, one kiwifruit
L606: per d was sufficient to achieve ‘healthy’ plasma levels of vitamin C (i.e.
L607: >50 µmol/l) and with two or three per d, ‘optimal’ plasma levels of the vitamin were
L608: reached. Our data confirm the pharmacokinetic data of Levine  et
L609: al. ( 
L610:  15 
L611:  , 
L612:  16 
L613:  )  and indicate that plasma vitamin C levels in humans saturate at an
L614: intake of about 200 mg/d (equivalent to approximately two kiwifruit per d), whereas
L615: leucocytes saturate at an intake of about 100 mg/d (equivalent to about one kiwifruit per
L616: d). This may therefore suggest that vitamin C supplementation is equivalent to consumption
L617: of the vitamin in foods. Finally, our study indicated that in order to observe a consistent
L618: effect of supplementation, it is critical to obtain a study population that has low initial
L619: vitamin C levels.
